  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWednesday night through Monday Assessment...a certainty (>98%) of \nmuch cooler conditions for the first part of the weekend. \nProbability of frost Friday night remains around 60-70%.\n\nThe next round of rain with embedded thunderstorms will occur \nWednesday night into Thursday evening. A tropical connection with \nthe Gulf and moisture coming off of Simon will increase PWATs into \nthe 1.5 to 2 inch range. These PWAT values have the potential of \nmeeting or exceeding the climatological max for October 15th. Thus \nheavier and efficient rainfall will be possible. The areas currently \nfavored for heavier rainfall are south of I-80, where better \nmoisture and forcing can be found. Overall rainfall accumulation \nshould largely remain <1.00\", with ensemble guidance highlighting \nareas south of Interstate 80 in a 30-50% chance of seeing an \ninch or more of rainfall. \n\nWindy conditions will be seen late Thursday night and Friday behind \nthe cold front. Winds atop of the mixed layer are 30-35 knots, which \nwould give wind gusts to at least 35 mph on Friday. We will continue \nto watch this for potential headlines. The strongest winds will be \nseen overnight Thursday through Friday AM, with gradual \ndecreases in speeds through the afternoon.\n\nCold Canadian high pressure will settle over the area Friday night \nbringing light winds and very chilly temperatures. Given the \nnegative delta on temperatures, the probability of frost has \nincreased to 60-70 percent. Headlines for frost are possible \nlater in the week.\n\nA gradual warm-up commences late this weekend into early next week \nwith temperatures trending to and then above normal.",
